# Caddy + caddy-l4 (layer4) + caddy-ratelimit, custom-built via xcaddy.
#
# PURPOSE 1 — TURN-TLS SNI split on :443. Stock Caddy has no way to inspect the
# raw TLS ClientHello SNI before termination, so a bare Caddy on this box
# cannot divert only the TURN hostname (dev.turn.matrix.inblock.io /
# turn.matrix.inblock.io) to LiveKit's plaintext external_tls listener while
# leaving every other :443 vhost untouched. caddy-l4's `layer4` Caddyfile
# app + `listener_wrappers` adds that SNI-based demux ahead of the `tls`
# wrapper. See docs/superpowers/plans/2026-08-03-turn-tls-edge-termination.md
# for the full design (Caddyfile.dev-aquafire carries the actual
# `servers :443 { listener_wrappers { layer4 { ... } tls } }` block that
# consumes this image).
#
# PURPOSE 2 — edge rate limiting for siwx-oidc's PUBLIC `GET /resolve`. That
# route is unauthenticated by design and costs up to two Synapse probes per
# request, and its own module doc (siwx-oidc `src/resolve.rs`) delegates the
# limiter here on purpose: "a rate limiter belongs at the reverse proxy that
# already fronts this service (Caddy), where it can be shaped per-deployment
# without a release. Do not add an in-process one here". Caddy ships NO
# rate-limiting directive of any kind, so that delegation is unimplementable
# without this module. The zones live in the `dev.siwx.inblock.io` /
# `siwx-oidc.inblock.io` site blocks of Caddyfile.dev-aquafire /
# Caddyfile.production.
#
# DEPLOY ORDER — IMAGE FIRST, and be precise about what is new here.
#
# Both Caddyfiles ALREADY required this custom image before rate limiting
# existed: each carries a `layer4` block, and stock `caddy:2-alpine` rejects
# them identically before and after this change (verified: same error, same
# line, on `git show HEAD:Caddyfile.production` and on the edited file). So
# "fails on stock Caddy" is not the new risk.
#
# The NEW risk is narrower and more likely: the image currently DEPLOYED
# (`caddy-l4:2.11.4-l4v0.1.2` — layer4 only, no ratelimit) parses today's
# config fine and will REJECT the new one. A `caddy reload` ahead of the image
# bump therefore takes down every vhost on the box — 8 on dev, 12 on prod, not
# just siwx-oidc. Rebuild and pull this image, THEN deploy the Caddyfile.
# Same shape as the Synapse-then-publisher ordering in siwx-oidc's CLAUDE.md,
# and for the same reason: the consumer of a capability must never arrive
# before the capability.
#
# This is also why Caddyfile.local and Caddyfile.e2e carry NEITHER block —
# both run stock `caddy:2-alpine` (docker-compose.local.yml,
# docker-compose.e2e.yml), so adding one would break local dev and the e2e
# harness.
#
# VERSION-LOCK RATIONALE — caddy-l4 (verified 2026-08-03 against upstream):
#   caddy-l4 v0.1.2's go.mod pins `github.com/caddyserver/caddy/v2 v2.11.4`
#   EXACTLY (not >=, not a range) — https://github.com/mholt/caddy-l4/blob/
#   v0.1.2/go.mod. xcaddy statically links whatever `caddy` module version is
#   resolved at build time, so the builder base image's Caddy version and the
#   plugin's pinned Caddy version must match, or `xcaddy build` either fails
#   to resolve or silently pulls in a second, inconsistent copy of caddy/v2.
#   `caddy:2.11.4-builder` is therefore not an arbitrary "latest stable" pick;
#   it is the specific tag that satisfies caddy-l4 v0.1.2's exact pin.
#
# VERSION-LOCK RATIONALE — caddy-ratelimit (verified 2026-09-13 BY BUILDING,
# not by reading):
#   Pinned to the COMMIT 5625512f24f6f59d6f64fb3aafe5eecff0b286db (master
#   head, 2026-06-12), deliberately NOT to the only existing tag.
#
#   * The repo's ONLY tag is v0.1.0 (2024-08-28); its go.mod requires
#     caddy/v2 v2.8.0. The pinned commit's go.mod requires v2.10.0. BOTH are
#     LOWER than the builder's 2.11.4, and Go's minimal-version-selection
#     resolves the MAXIMUM of all requirements, so caddy/v2 stays at exactly
#     2.11.4 and caddy-l4's exact requirement is unaffected. The skew this
#     file exists to prevent is a module requiring a caddy/v2 HIGHER than the
#     builder ships (that silently upgrades Caddy under the other plugin);
#     neither candidate does. Proven in the built binary:
#         dep github.com/caddyserver/caddy/v2  v2.11.4
#         dep github.com/mholt/caddy-l4        v0.1.2
#         dep github.com/mholt/caddy-ratelimit v0.1.1-0.20260612195517-5625512f24f6
#     (`docker run --rm <image> caddy build-info | grep caddy-`), and
#     `caddy version` reports v2.11.4.
#
#   * Both candidates BUILD and RUN against caddy 2.11.4 — each was built
#     against caddy-l4 v0.1.2 on caddy:2.11.4-builder and each produced a
#     working binary listing `layer4` AND `http.handlers.rate_limit`. The
#     tag is therefore NOT rejected for incompatibility; it compiles fine.
#
#   * The commit is taken anyway because v0.1.0's Caddyfile parser has no
#     `default:` case in its subdirective switch, so it SILENTLY IGNORES any
#     subdirective it does not recognise (upstream fixed this in #80,
#     2025-03-17, after the tag). Falsified here, not assumed: a config whose
#     zone carries `ipv6_prefix 64` (a feature added after v0.1.0) plus a
#     deliberate typo `log_ky` validates as **"Valid configuration"** on
#     v0.1.0 and fails with **"unrecognized subdirective 'log_ky'"** on the
#     pinned commit. A rate limiter that is silently not the rate limiter you
#     wrote is worse than no rate limiter, because the config reads as
#     protection. The pinned commit also carries the reload fix for
#     `caddy_rate_limit_*` metrics (#102) — without it the counters read 0
#     after the first `caddy reload`, and a `caddy reload` is exactly how
#     both of these Caddyfiles are deployed.
#
#   * Upstream is alive but does not tag (last release 2024-08, last commit
#     2026-06-12). Prefer a real tag the moment one lands after 5625512;
#     re-check that its go.mod's caddy/v2 requirement is still <= the builder
#     tag before switching.
#
# BUMP WARNING: if you bump any version here, you MUST re-check the pin
# pairing first (read the target caddy-l4 tag's go.mod for its
# `caddyserver/caddy/v2` requirement, and the target caddy-ratelimit tag's)
# and update BOTH the builder tag below and the final-stage tag to match,
# each with its new index digest.
# Bumping just one, or bumping to a plugin tag whose go.mod requires a
# different Caddy version than the builder ships, reintroduces the exact skew
# this pin exists to prevent.
#
# PROOF COMMAND (both modules present in the binary):
#   docker build -f dockerfiles/Dockerfile.caddy-l4 -t caddy-l4-rl:test .
#   docker run --rm caddy-l4-rl:test caddy list-modules | grep -E 'layer4|rate_limit'
#     -> caddy.listeners.layer4
#        http.handlers.rate_limit
#        layer4 (+ its matchers/handlers)

# Both stages are pinned by tag AND multi-arch index digest, so a re-pushed
# tag cannot change the Caddy we build with or ship. Resolve a digest with
#   skopeo inspect --raw docker://docker.io/library/caddy:<tag> | sha256sum
# The Go modules below need no digest: the builder leaves Go's defaults on, so
# each module version (the commit pin included) is checked against the public
# checksum database, sum.golang.org, and always resolves to the same source.

# ---- build stage ----
FROM caddy:2.11.4-builder@sha256:369218c81ca6d6af249981221b3a5c764d886dd5b058f51d144066de13f2418d AS builder
RUN xcaddy build \
    --with github.com/mholt/caddy-l4@v0.1.2 \
    --with github.com/mholt/caddy-ratelimit@5625512f24f6f59d6f64fb3aafe5eecff0b286db
# ---- final stage ----
FROM caddy:2.11.4@sha256:0c994536bddb66445885237f1a5dcc1916bccea922661c76b4e9fc24061f9b52
COPY --from=builder /usr/bin/caddy /usr/bin/caddy
# Published from this repository like the Synapse and Element images, so it
# carries the same LICENSE and NOTICE at the same path.
COPY LICENSE NOTICE /usr/share/licenses/siwx-oidc-matrix-server/
